If your business has a Facebook page with a few hundred followers, it's easy to feel like your online presence is sorted. But here's the thing: you don't own that platform. Facebook could change its algorithm, restrict your reach, or shut your page down tomorrow — and there would be nothing you could do about it.

A website is something you own. And for a small business trying to attract local customers, that distinction matters more than most people realise.

## Social media and websites do different jobs

Social media is great for staying in front of people who already know you. It keeps existing customers engaged, lets you post updates, and gives your business a personality.

But it's not where people go when they need a service right now. When someone needs a plumber, a roofer, or a local accountant, they go to Google — not Facebook. If you don't have a website showing up in those results, you're invisible at the exact moment someone is ready to buy.

## You don't control social media — they do

Your Facebook page sits on Facebook's platform, under Facebook's rules. That means:

- They can reduce your organic reach whenever they want (and they have, repeatedly)
- Your page could be reported, restricted, or removed without warning
- You can't control what ads appear alongside your content
- One algorithm update can wipe out visibility you've spent years building

A website, on the other hand, is yours. Your domain, your content, your rules.

## Websites build trust in a way social media doesn't

Most people expect a legitimate business to have a proper website. When someone Googles your business name and finds only a Facebook page, it raises questions — intentionally or not.

A clean, professional website with clear services, pricing, and contact details signals that you're established. That trust converts into enquiries.

## Google doesn't rank your Facebook page

If you want to appear when someone types "plumber Merthyr Tydfil" or "roofer Cardiff", you need a website with pages optimised for those searches. Social media posts don't rank in Google the way website pages do.

## Getting online doesn't have to be expensive

The most common reason small businesses stick to social media is cost. A custom-built website can feel like a big investment.

That's exactly why our [Managed Starter Website](/services/managed-starter-websites/) exists. From £20/month inc. VAT, you get a professionally built website with your domain, hosting, and ongoing management included — plus 5, 10, or unlimited new pages added every month depending on your tier. These pages sit outside any SEO plan. No large upfront cost. No technical knowledge required.

One new customer from your website typically covers months of that cost. It stops being an expense and becomes an asset.

## Use both — but build toward owned

You don't have to choose between social media and a website. Use both. Post on Facebook and Instagram to stay visible to your existing audience. But build your website as the long-term foundation — the asset that works for you 24/7, ranks on Google, and doesn't depend on anyone else's platform.

